    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Role in cor pulmonale:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " tx is directed at the cause; acute causes incl massive pulmonary embolization. When the cause is acute PE: initial, then maintenance anticoagulation is indicated (prevents further embolization and new clot); initial choices incl IV UFH and SC LMWH."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "PLT:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " check b4 and periodically during heparin; thrombocytopenia occurs in up to 30%, 2–20 days (avg 5–9) after starting. If PLT fall below 100,000 or thrombosis recurs, stop heparin and evaluate for HIT; HIT can occur up to several wks after heparin = stopped."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Enoxaparin w CrCl < 30 mL/min:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " tx doses become 1 mg/kg SC QD."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Boxed warning (LMWH):"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " epidural or spinal hematomas w neuraxial anesthesia or spinal puncture, which may cause long-term or permanent paralysis."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Protamine dosing:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " each mg neutralizes about 100 units of UFH, ⍉more than 50 mg in any 10 minutes, given very slowly; for enoxaparin, 1 mg per 1 mg, and anti-Xa activity = never fully neutralized (max about 60%)."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
